me homologous to polyketide biosynthetic genes.\n\nDNA sequence : the journal of DNA sequencing and mapping\n\nPMID: 7987007\n\nPublication date: 1994\n\nPublication status: ppublish\n\nJournal abbreviation: DNA Seq\n\nJournal country: England\n\nJournal NLM unique ID: 9107800\n\nJournal linking ISSN: 1026-7913\n\nISSN: 1042-5179\n\nVolume: 4\n\nIssue: 4\n\nPages: 219-29\n\n## Identifiers\n\n  * DOI: 10.3109/10425179409020845\n\n## Authors\n\n  * M Piecq\n  * P Dehottay\n  * A Biot\n  * J Dusart\n\n## Affiliations\n\n  * Centre d'Ingénierie des Protéines, Université de Liège, Belgium.\n\n## Languages\n\n  * eng\n\n## Publication types\n\n  * Comparative Study\n  * Journal Article\n  * Research Support, Non-U.S. Gov't\n\n## Abstract\n\nThe actinomycete Kibdelosporangium aridum naturally produces ardacin, a new glycopeptide antibiotic, the biosynthetic pathway of which should involve the participation of a polyketide synthase (PKS). A K. aridum 2.9 kb BamHI genomic fragment homologous to actI (a locus of the PKS cluster catalyzing polyketide chain assembly for actinorhodin biosynthesis in Streptomyces coelicolor) was isolated by shotgun cloning. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPoly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid] | Krackeler Scientific, Inc. (https://www.krackeler.com/catalog/sigma/ALDRICH/363502)\nciteturn16search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; ## ALDRICH/363502 - natural origin ... Application:  | Poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid (PHB) is a biodegradable and biocompatible polymer produced by several bacterial species, owing to its low resorbability and tunable properties, it may find uses in a variety of applications such as tissue engineering, controlled release systems etc.\n\n# Poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid]\n\n## ALDRICH/363502 - natural origin\n\nSynonym: (R)-3-Hydroxybutyric acid polymerized; PHB\n\nCAS Number: 29435-48-1\nMDL Number: MFCD00134263\nLinear Formula: [COCH_{2}CH(CH_{3})O]_{n}\nProduct Type: Chemical\nCatalog Number  | PKG Qty.  | Price  | Quantity  | \n--- | --- | --- | --- | ---\n45-363502-10G  | 10 g\n | $193.00\n1/EA  | [Input]  | Add To Favorites\n45-363502-100G  | 100 g\n | $1140.00\n1/EA  | [Input]  | Add To Favorites\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://journals.asm.org/doi/reader/tables/10.1128/cmr.00002-11)\nciteturn16search5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Antibiotic  | Purpose  | Antimicrobial class  | Spectrum of activity  | Use in human medicine  | Structurally related antibiotic(s)/antibiotic(s) with shared cross-resistance  | Comments^{c} ... Ardacin  | Bovine AGP  | Glycopeptides  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Vancomycin, teicoplanin  | Withdrawn from EU in 1997; not licensed in U.S. ... Carbomycin^{b}  | Respiratory disease prevention and treatment in poultry  | Macrolides  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Other macrolides  |\nsubtitles Table 1.\n\nAntibiotic  | Purpose  | Antimicrobial class  | Spectrum of activity  | Use in human medicine  | Structurally related antibiotic(s)/antibiotic(s) with shared cross-resistance  | Comments^{c}\n--- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nArdacin  | Bovine AGP  | Glycopeptides  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Vancomycin, teicoplanin  | Withdrawn from EU in 1997; not licensed in U.S.\nAmoxicillin,^{b} ampicillin^{b}  | Aquaculture, oral treatment of swine colibacillosis, treatment of bovine bacterial enteritis and subclinical mastitis  | Aminopenicillins  | Moderate  | Yes  | All penicillins  |  \nAvilamycin  | AGP for broilers  | Orthosomysins  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Everninomycin  | Withdrawn from EU; not licensed in U.S.\nAvoparcin^{b}  | AGP  | Glycopeptides  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Vancomycin, teicoplanin  | Withdrawn from EU in 1997; not licensed in U.S.\nBacitracin/zinc bacitracin  | AGP for poultry, beef cattle, and swine; control of swine dysentery and bacterial enteritis; control of poultry enteritis  | Polypeptides  | Gram-positive organisms  | Yes (zinc bacitracin)  | Actinomycin, colistin, polymyxin B  | Withdrawn from EU in 1999; available in U.S.; used in Japan\nBambermycin  | AGP for poultry, swine, and cattle  | Phosphoglycolipids  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Vancomycin, teicoplanin  | Withdrawn from EU in 2006; available in U.S.\nCarbadox  | Control of swine dysentery  | Quinoxalines  | Gram-positive and -negative organisms  | No  | Other quinoxolines  | Withdrawn due to worker toxicity in EU and Canada; available in U.S. and Mexico\nCarbomycin^{b}  | Respiratory disease prevention and treatment in poultry  | Macrolides  | Gram-positive organisms  | No  | Other macrolides  |  \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSupplement to Vol. 40, No. 1, 1986 (https://cdn.who.int/media/docs/default-source/international-nonproprietary-names-%28inn%29/pl57.pdf?download=true)\nciteturn16search14 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 23.0 years ago; Chemical structural formula for ardacin (ardacinum).\n\nThe main structure is a complex glycopeptide-like molecule containing multiple aromatic rings, ether linkages, amide bonds and hydroxyl groups.\nSupplement to Vol. 40, No. 1, 1986\n\n**Proposed International Nonproprietary Names (Prop. INN): List 55**\n\n**p. 2 — ardacinum / ardacin**\n\nAdd the following graphic formula:\n\n<visual_element id=\"e1\">\nChemical structural formula for ardacin (ardacinum).\n\nThe main structure is a complex glycopeptide-like molecule containing multiple aromatic rings, ether linkages, amide bonds and hydroxyl groups.\n\nVisible structural labels and groups include:\n- Multiple aromatic rings, including rings substituted with Cl and OH.\n- Ether linkages (O).\n- Hydroxyl groups (OH, HO).\n- Carboxyl groups (HOOC).\n- Amide groups (NH, N, C=O).\n- Methyl group (CH₃).\n- Multiple interconnected aromatic and amino-acid-like structural units.\n\nA separate structure below the main formula is labeled **(side chain)**. It contains:\n- COOH\n- OH groups\n- O\n- NH\n- NH—C(=O)—(CH₂)ₙ—CH₃\n\nThe side-chain structure is shown separately beneath the main molecular structure.\n</visual_element>\n\n**p. 17 — tetronasinum / tetronasin**\n\nReplace molecular formula by: **C₃₅H₅₄O₈**\n\n**p. 20 — omoconazolum / omoconazole**\n\nDelete 4991 rev. and insert the following CAS reg. no.: **105102-19-0**\n\nPage iii--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPoly[-3-hydroxybutyric acid], | 363502-10G | SIGMA-ALDRICH | SLS (https://www.scientificlabs.com/en/product/polymer-other/363502-10G)\nciteturn16search6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 4 weeks ago; Code: 363502-10G D2-231 ... Poly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid (PHB) is a biodegradable and biocompatible polymer produced by several bacterial species, owing to its low resorbability and tunable properties, it may find uses in a variety of applications such as tissue engineering, controlled release systems etc.\n\n# Poly[-3-hydroxybutyric acid], natural origin\n\nCode: 363502-10G D2-231\n\nNot available outside of the UK & Ireland.\n\nApplication\n\nPoly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid (PHB) is a biodegradable and biocompatible polymer produced by several bacterial species, owing to its low resorbability a...\n\n[Input] ADD\n\nYour Price\n\n£164.06 10G\n\nREQUEST\n\n  * REQUEST A QUOTE\n\n  * OVERVIEW\n  * SPECIFICATIONS\n  * DOCUMENTS\n  * RANGE\n  * ATTRIBUTES\n\nNot available outside of the UK & Ireland.\n\nApplication\n\nPoly[(R)-3-hydroxybutyric acid (PHB) is a biodegradable and biocompatible polymer produced by several bacterial species, owing to its low resorbability and tunable properties, it may find uses in a variety of applications such as tissue engineering, controlled release systems etc.\n\nGeneral description\n\nBiocompatible, biodegradable polymer.\n\nPackaging\n\n10, 100 g in poly bottle\n\nQuality Level  | 200\nSMILES string  | CC(O)CC(O)=O\ntransition temp  | T_{m} 172 °C (DSC)\n\nSafety Data Sheet\n  * Safety Data Sheet for 363502-10G\nCertificate of Analysis\n  * Sigma Certificate of Analysis\n\nCode\n\nDescription\n\nUnit Size\n\nList Price\n\nQty\n\nCas Number  | 29435-48-1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAntimicrobial feed additives - del 3 (Statens offentliga utredningar 1997:132) | Sveriges riksdag (https://www.riksdagen.se/sv/dokument-och-lagar/dokument/statens-offentliga-utredningar/antimicrobial-feed-additives-del-3_glb3132d3/html/)\nciteturn16search7 [wordlim: 200] Published: 29.9 years ago; Crawled: last month; Ardacin and avoparcin are glycopeptide antibiotics. ... The basic peptide structure possesses a nucleus of seven amino acid residues and five amino acids. ... This will allow the bacteria to grow in presence of the antibiotic.\nAnnex D: Ardacin and avoparcin\n\nD.1 Introduction\n\nArdacin and avoparcin are glycopeptide antibiotics. The glycopeptides are\n\nlarge molecules produced by a variety of bacterial genera including\n\n3TREPTOMYCES, !CTINOPLANES .OCARDIA and +IBDELOSPORANGIUM. Avoparcin and ardacin are both compounds of two or more substances with similar molecular structure.\n\nChemically, the glycopeptides all have a common core and differ in the four side chains (figure D.I). The basic peptide structure possesses a nucleus of seven amino acid residues and five amino acids. Sugars and amino sugars, linked to the core structure, are mainly located on the outside of the molecule. They do not markedly affect the antimicrobial activity, but give the substances different pharmacokinetic properties (Reynolds, 1990).\n\nGlycopeptides are active against gram-positive bacteria such as\n\nstaphylococci, streptococci, enterococci, corynebacteria, clostridia and ,ISTERIA spp. Presently known glycopeptides are not active against gram-\n\nnegative bacteria because the antibiotic molecules are unable to pass through the outer membrane and hence cannot reach their target (Reynolds, 1